@page "/HowItWorks/section-104-pool"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/section-104-pool">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Shares that the <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">same-day</a> and
                    <a href="HowItWorks/30-day-rule" class="link-styled">30-day</a> rules do not use go into one pool per
                    share. The pool's cost includes purchase fees, so it is an average cost.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A disposal matched with the pool takes cost = pool cost × shares sold ÷ shares in the pool, so the
                    average cost per share does not change.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The app works through the pool in date order. Corporate actions that change the pool, such as
                    <a href="HowItWorks/stock-splits" class="link-styled">stock splits</a>,
                    <a href="HowItWorks/takeovers-and-spin-offs" class="link-styled">takeovers and spin-offs</a>,
                    <a href="HowItWorks/return-of-capital" class="link-styled">return of capital</a>,
                    <a href="HowItWorks/partner-transfers" class="link-styled">transfers with a spouse or civil partner</a> and
                    <a href="HowItWorks/reporting-funds" class="link-styled">excess reportable income and equalisation</a>,
                    are applied on their dates, between the trades.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Every change to the pool is recorded, so you can follow the running quantity and cost on the
                    <a href="Section104DataPage" class="link-styled">Section 104 Data</a> page.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example</h2>
                <p>
                    You buy 100 shares for £990 plus a £10 fee (£1,000), then 200 shares for £2,590 plus a £10 fee (£2,600).
                    The pool holds 300 shares costing £3,600, which is £12 a share.
                </p>
                <p>
                    You then sell 150 shares for £2,250 less a £10 fee, so the net proceeds are £2,240. The allowable cost is
                    £3,600 × 150/300 = £1,800, giving a <strong>gain of £440</strong>.
                </p>
                <p class="mb-0">The pool is left with 150 shares costing £1,800, still £12 a share.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://cgtvisualiser.co.uk/guides/section-104-pool/" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CGT Visualiser: Section 104 pool</a> (independent site)</li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/stock-splits" class="link-styled">Stock splits</a>, <a href="HowItWorks/takeovers-and-spin-offs" class="link-styled">takeovers and spin-offs</a> and <a href="HowItWorks/return-of-capital" class="link-styled">return of capital</a>: how corporate actions change the pool</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/104" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s104</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
